The C program is 5 lines, but nearly all of its observable behavior lives in the undefined and failure paths, so the Rust reimplements those explicitly rather than using Rust's conveniences:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- **scanf semantics.** A hand-rolled byte scanner with one byte of push-back mimics [38;5;10m%d[0m: skip whitespace (including newlines), optional sign, digits, stop at the first non-digit and leave it unconsumed. Because the C ignores scanf's return value, a partial or failed conversion must leave the initial values intact — input [38;5;10m42[0m yields [38;5;10mquotient: 42, remainder: 0[0m since [38;5;10my[0m stays [38;5;10m1[0m, and [38;5;10mabc[0m yields [38;5;10m1 / 1[0m.[0m[0m
- **Integer overflow in scanf.** glibc converts [38;5;10m%d[0m via [38;5;10mstrtol[0m, so an out-of-range literal saturates to [38;5;10mLONG_MIN[0m/[38;5;10mLONG_MAX[0m and is then truncated to [38;5;10mint[0m. That is why [38;5;10m4294967297[0m becomes [38;5;10m1[0m and a 23-digit number becomes [38;5;10m-1[0m. I verified this against the compiled C rather than assuming it, and reproduced it with i128 accumulation, saturation at the [38;5;10mlong[0m boundary, then an [38;5;10mas i32[0m truncation.[0m[0m
- **div by zero and [38;5;10mINT_MIN / -1[0m.** Both are UB in C and trap as SIGFPE on x86-64 with no output. Rust's [38;5;10m/[0m would instead panic with a message on stderr and exit 101, so [38;5;10mc_div[0m issues the real [38;5;10mcdq; idiv[0m via [38;5;10masm![0m (without [38;5;10mpure[0m, so the trap can't be optimized away). This is the one unsafe block; everything else is safe Rust. Non-x86-64 targets fall back to checked operators, which cannot reproduce the trap — noted in a comment.[0m[0m
